@node Layers
@section Layers
@cindex layers

@example
   HTTP/3, DoQ, ...                (applications, not in this tree)
   ---------------------------------------------------------------
   endpoint    many connections behind one socket: routing, admission
   quic        connection, streams, flow control, recovery, migration
   tls / dtls  handshake engines and record layers (1.3 and 1.2)
   codec       varint, frame, packet, transport parameters, handshake msgs
   ---------------------------------------------------------------
   crypto      hashes, HMAC, HKDF, AEAD, header protection, KEM   (libgcrypt)
   trust       X.509 loading and chain validation                 (GnuTLS)
   policy      the closed list of what may be negotiated
@end example

Upper layers see only @file{crypto.h}, @file{trust.h} and
@file{policy.h}.  Nothing above @file{crypto.c} and @file{trust.c}
includes @file{<gcrypt.h>} or @file{<gnutls/@dots{}>}.

@node Crypto
@section Crypto: libgcrypt only
@cindex libgcrypt
@cindex post-quantum

@file{src/crypto.c} wraps libgcrypt and adds only protocol glue defined
by RFCs: HKDF-Extract and HKDF-Expand (RFC 5869, built on libgcrypt HMAC
because TLS needs extract and expand separately), HKDF-Expand-Label
(RFC 8446 section 7.1) and the QUIC key schedule (RFC 9001 section 5).
Each is checked against the vectors in its RFC (@file{tests/test-crypto.c}).
No primitive is implemented in this tree.

Findings from probing the installed libgcrypt 1.12.4:

@itemize @bullet
@item
ML-KEM-512, 768 and 1024 are implemented (@code{gcry_kem_*}), so the
hybrid key exchange groups @code{X25519MLKEM768},
@code{SecP256r1MLKEM768} and @code{SecP384r1MLKEM1024} (RFC 10024) can be
built from libgcrypt alone: classical ECDH plus ML-KEM, combined as the
RFC specifies.

@item
ML-DSA has a reserved algorithm ID (@code{GCRY_PK_MLDSA}) but no
implementation, and GnuTLS 3.8.13 declares ML-DSA-44, 65 and 87 but the
installed build has no backend for them.  Neither library gives
post-quantum signatures on the development machine.  The policy lists
the ML-DSA schemes, the signature module reports
@code{GQ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE} for them, and they become available when a
backend provides them.  Post-quantum key exchange is unaffected.

@item
ECDH for X25519, secp256r1 and secp384r1 is done through libgcrypt's KEM
interface (@code{GCRY_KEM_RAW_*}), which fits TLS key shares exactly.
For the NIST curves libgcrypt returns the whole shared point and TLS uses
the x coordinate only.  Invalid peer points are rejected by libgcrypt.

@item
X25519, Ed25519, Ed448, AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305 and HKDF are all
present.
@end itemize

If a second library is ever required for post-quantum support it goes
behind @file{crypto.h} as another backend, selected at configure time,
and must be licence-compatible with the LGPL version 3 or later.

@node Trust
@section Trust: X.509 behind an interface
@cindex certificates
@cindex GnuTLS

Gumdrop delegates certificate validation to the JVM and Hopf uses webpki
and the platform roots.  In C the equivalent is
@file{include/gnuquic/trust.h}: a trust store, and
@code{gq_trust_verify_chain} taking DER certificates and an optional host
name.  The first implementation calls GnuTLS (LGPL version 2.1 or later,
which is compatible).  Because callers only see @code{gq_trust}, a later
in-tree implementation (ASN.1 DER decoding, RFC 5280 path validation,
RFC 9525 name matching) can replace it without touching the engines.

The validation policy is fixed.  SHA-1 and MD5 signature chains fail,
RSA keys under 2048 bits fail, dates are always checked, and the leaf must
be usable for the stated purpose (TLS server or client authentication).

@node Key exchange and signatures
@section Key exchange and signatures

@file{kx.h} is the key exchange facade: @code{gq_kx_generate} (client),
@code{gq_kx_respond} (server) and @code{gq_kx_complete} (client) for all
six groups in the policy, with the hybrid concatenation orders of
RFC 10024.  It uses libgcrypt only.

@file{sign.h} is the signature facade for CertificateVerify: load a PEM
private key, take the public key from a peer's certificate, and sign and
verify with the policy's schemes, checking that scheme and key type fit
(ECDSA schemes bind the curve).  Signatures go through GnuTLS because it
already parses the keys and certificates; the interface hides that, as
@file{trust.h} does.  RSA under 2048 bits is refused everywhere.

@node The push-parser contract
@section The push-parser contract
@cindex push parser

The pattern is taken from Gumdrop's @code{H2Parser} and
@code{QuicFrameParser}, Hopf's @code{ProtocolHandler::receive} and the
@code{underflow} flag of jprotobuf:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Input arrives as chunks.  A parser never blocks or reads a descriptor.

@item
A parser takes @code{(const uint8_t **buf, size_t *len)}, consumes
complete units and advances both.  The caller keeps the tail and
re-presents it with more data (@code{GQ_NEED_MORE}).

@item
One forward pass, events to a handler, payloads as slices into the
caller's buffer, no object graphs and no whole-message buffering.

@item
Units are all-or-nothing, so no event fires for a half-received frame.
@end itemize

@code{gq_frame_parse} is the model: a @code{gq_frame} tagged union is
delivered transiently on the stack.  ACK ranges are not expanded into an
array; the event carries the validated raw range section and
@code{gq_ack_foreach} walks it.  Tests run the same corpus whole and in
every chunk size from 1 to 64.

Engines (TLS, DTLS, and the QUIC connection) follow the reactive style
of Gumdrop's @code{HandshakeEngine} and Hopf's @code{TlsEngine}: feed
input in, receive events out through a sink structure of callbacks, with
explicit state enumerations.  The caller pumps them.  No engine performs
I/O.

